## Run

    mise trust
    mise install
    mise run up

Each backend is an instance with its own origin, ports and database and, with identity, its own Kratos (with its own database) and session key. The backends share one Postgres server, `acme-postgres` on host port 10132: every backend and every Kratos has its own database and user on it, created by `acme-postgres-init` on every `up`. Host ports 10100-10199, bound to `127.0.0.1`.

- acme-srv-first: http://localhost:10100; ports srv 10100, postgres 10132, kratos-public 10133
- acme-srv-second: http://localhost:10120 (console acme-web); ports srv 10101, postgres 10132, kratos-public 10143

Defaults are local-only placeholders. Per-instance overrides go in `.deploy/local/<component>/.env` (optional, keys as in the `.example.env` beside it). `.deploy/local/.env` is read only by compose interpolation and reaches every instance.
Without mise: `docker compose -f .deploy/local/docker-compose.yml --profile app up -d --build`.
Stop with `mise run down` (volumes kept). `.deploy/local/README.md` lists every port and service.
The compose project is `acme-101` (`<project name>-<port band>`). Two local stacks keep separate containers and volumes when their project name or port band differs; to run both at the same time their port bands must differ too. Projects with the same name and band share one compose project (containers, Postgres volume), and their host ports clash. Give each project its own name or port band.
On `localhost` one browser profile is signed in to one identity instance at a time: signing in to the second overwrites the first's cookies. Use a second browser profile or a private window per instance.

## Edit a component on the host

Stop its container, then run it on the host on the same port (`mise run infra` starts the infrastructure alone). Per instance:

Editing acme-srv-first:

    docker compose -f .deploy/local/docker-compose.yml stop acme-srv-first
    mise run dev:acme-srv-first

Editing acme-web:

    docker compose -f .deploy/local/docker-compose.yml stop acme-web
    mise run dev:acme-web

Editing acme-srv-second runs acme-web on the host too (nginx in the container reaches only the acme-srv-second container); run each task in its own terminal:

    docker compose -f .deploy/local/docker-compose.yml stop acme-srv-second acme-web
    mise run dev:acme-srv-second
    mise run dev:acme-web

Once per clone: `npm --prefix acme-web ci`.

## TCK

Once per clone: `npm --prefix specs ci`. One task per backend:

acme-srv-first: tags @core, @identity, @otel.

    mise run tck:acme-srv-first

acme-srv-second: tags @core, @feature-flags, @identity, @otel. Add `APP_FLAGS_FILE=../specs/feature-flags/tck/fixtures/flags.yaml` to `.deploy/local/acme-srv-second/.env` (acme-srv-second reads it in both modes), then run `mise run up` again or restart `mise run dev:acme-srv-second`.

    mise run tck:acme-srv-second
